Z||000000||Adrift||11-19-2001||07:05 PM||||Should my headlight work if I only connect the bulb, and not the motor or anything else?<BR>If the answers yes, what could be reasons why they don't work? (I just bought Xenon-type kit from ebay (the $39 one) and hooked it up to my project car, just by pulling the plug out of ones installed and plugging into the new casing/bulb and got no light)||||reg||1
Z||000001||kempo||11-19-2001||07:12 PM||||Check your fuse, it may be the culpret.  I am interested in getting a set of those Xenon kits as well.  Please let me know your findings.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Adrift||11-19-2001||07:17 PM||||any other suggestions? Fuse seems to be ok||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||11-19-2001||08:24 PM||||I don't think the headlight socket cares whether or not the motors are hooked up.  I assume the existing headlights in the "project car" light up okay?  If so, the new headlights (or the way they are connected) are the real problem (duh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).<P>Also, I don't think the headlights are protected by a fuse, but rather a circuit breaker.  Is it possible the new bulb draws significantly more amps than the baseline headlights, and it's tripping the breaker (it's a self-resetting thermal breaker, I think)?<P>I'll quit now - since you've probably had  enough of what I "don't think..."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">        ||||reg||5
